## Result

  {"id": "<row id>",
   "status": "ok" | "unsure" | "relative" | "unavailable",
   "measurements": [{"measurement": "<plain decimal coefficient>", "unit": "<unit as read>"}]}

Only "ok" carries measurements; all other statuses return [].

  ok           at least one point quantity that measures the canonical endpoint, including an endpoint-defined ratio, percentage, change, or normalized readout
  relative     a real number referenced to a formulation, fed/fasted state, dose arm, treatment, baseline, control, comparator drug, or other experiment-specific denominator not defined by the endpoint
  unsure       the endpoint quantity or its unit is unclear, or only a bound, range, fraction, or conflicting same-unit values are given
  unavailable  no numeric measurement of the canonical endpoint; digits describe conditions, doses, times, sample sizes, genes, tables, or another outcome

"unsure" and "relative" are valid answers. Prefer them over guessing.

The number must measure the canonical endpoint itself. For example, an assay concentration or dose is unavailable under permeability, solubility, clearance, metabolism, or stability. An IC50 is unavailable under a substrate-status claim unless the endpoint itself is inhibition or potency. A percentage remaining is definitional under a stability or depletion readout; percent of control is relative under a plain concentration or exposure endpoint.

For oral exposure, AUC, Cmax, concentration, Tmax, and bioavailability, keep the named endpoint outcome only. A dose-normalized exposure is definitional when the endpoint names that normalization. Oral/IV bioavailability and an endpoint explicitly naming a ratio or comparison may define their denominator. Otherwise fed/fasted, formulation, treatment, dose-arm, and cross-drug comparisons are relative.

An `auc` or `cmax` endpoint alone does not define test/reference, fed/fasted, metabolite/parent, day/day, or treatment/control ratios; those remain relative. A physical AUC normalized to its own dose is absolute, including under an AUC endpoint. `dose_normalized_exposure` defines normalization by dose, but not a ratio between two regimens. A physical AUC, Cmax, permeability, clearance, or stability point remains absolute when merely reported for one named study arm.

For Fa, permeability, absorption, solubility, dissolution, and GI-stability measurements are distinct endpoints. For Fg, transport, bidirectional permeability, uptake, gut-wall extraction, and intestinal metabolism are distinct endpoints. For Fh, intrinsic/hepatic clearance, extraction ratio, half-life, depletion, microsomal/hepatocyte stability, CYP/UGT metabolism, and first-pass metabolism are distinct endpoints. Do not substitute a mechanistically related quantity for the named endpoint.

Under intestinal metabolism, a source-stated intestinal metabolic half-life or named gut-wall fraction (`Fg`, `Fa x Fg`, or `Fabs x Fg`) measures the endpoint; use unit `fraction` for the dimensionless fractions. A source-stated Papp or Peff point is an endpoint measurement even when its support also names an inhibitor, formulation, or other study condition. A metabolite formation amount or rate is not a parent-drug metabolic-stability value.

If a row states a relative claim and a separate absolute endpoint value, return the absolute value and ignore the comparison.

## Value and unit

`measurement` is one plain decimal coefficient: optional sign, digits, optional decimal point. Remove approximation around a point (`about 8` -> `8`) and spread (`8 +/- 1.5` -> `8`). Never turn `<0.1`, `>30%`, `10-20`, `up to 7`, or `about 1/10` into a point.

Do not return words, inequalities, arithmetic, fractions, `e` notation, or a power of ten in `measurement`. Put every source-stated scale in `unit`: `3.16 x 10^-6 cm/s` -> measurement `3.16`, unit `10^-6 cm/s`; `4.2e-7 cm/s` -> measurement `4.2`, unit `10^-7 cm/s`. Keep an already expanded decimal unchanged. Never infer a scale, convert, rescale, round, or compute.

`unit` is the shortest complete unit explicitly stated. Do not append route, tissue, species, condition, or endpoint descriptions. A percent, fraction, ratio, fold, percent remaining, or dose-normalized basis belongs in the unit when the source states it.
